ine Graham wins South Carolina GOP Senate runoff", "source": "Axios", "published": "2026-08-25T07:00:00+00:00", "url": "https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMikgFBVV95cUxPa2E3amZGN3pGVUNrelRCbTlkREtxQ3lFODJUcUtLdWZZVWowZVVETnYtRU9BRkZFcl9wZTNsMlZPdEtGWkhwU3BEVWVvd2Q1RHVxU2JpZkpPa2p1engxVGdnR3dYdnZEWFdBNURJZWlBcXZpaFNFdjNQQjd0VnhKTzNLTDZrTDFyTmZQVXNxV2h6UQ?oc=5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": -5.56, "prior_sd": 5.0, "poll_margin": -0.84, "poll_sd": 6.09, "poll_weight": 0.4, "fundamentals": {"margin": -2.81, "lean": -8.0, "environment": 7.38, "incumbency": -3.0, "money": 0.81}, "rating_margin": -7.4, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 6.23, "baseline_margin": -3.66, "analyst_adjustment": -0.34, "key_factors": ["Recent polls show a surprisingly tight race, but most are partisan-sponsored and low-quality", "Expert ratings lean toward Likely R, and prediction markets price Andrews around 15-20%", "GOP super PACs are shifting $5 million to defend Graham, signaling real concern but also resources", "Andrews has outraised Graham massively, yet incumbency and South Carolina's R+8 lean favor Graham"], "rationale": "The polling average sits near a one-point Republican edge, but the ratings consensus and prediction markets point to a clearer Graham advantage, and the fundamentals baseline of roughly -4 fits that picture. Andrews' fundraising and the GOP's emergency spending justify only a modest move from the ratings-implied margin, not a tossup call.", "watch": "Whether independent, non-partisan polls in October confirm the tight polling average or revert toward the ratings' Likely R view.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Darline Graham (R) is running."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's Senate race pairs interim appointee Darline Graham, an incumbent in a state that leans Republican by eight points, against Democrat Annie Andrews, a prolific fundraiser who has built a large cash advantage. Late-September surveys show a single-digit race, with some polls putting the candidates within a point, though several come from partisan sponsors. Expert raters mostly call the race Likely Republican, with a few at Lean Republican, and prediction markets give Andrews roughly a 15-20 percent chance.\n\nThe clearest signal of competitiveness is Republican anxiety: a top super PAC is redirecting $5 million to defend Graham, and a contentious Trump attack on Andrews has drawn wide coverage. Still, incumbency, the state's partisan lean, and outside spending give Graham the edge, with the race rated Lean Republican and meaningful uncertainty about how tight it truly is.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1565, "margin": -5.15}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2122, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2115, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2143, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1474, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2135, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2708, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2623, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.2878, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.2627, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.2896, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.2599, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.2566, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.194, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.1953, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-10-07", "p_dem": 0.2084, "margin": -4.0}]}